<article>
  <div class="article-header">
    <h1>Is Weed Legal in Maine?</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Yes — for adults 21+. Here is exactly what Maine law allows, and where it still does not.</p>
    <div class="byline">
      <p>By <a href="/about/authors#calvin-waters"><strong>Calvin Waters</strong></a>, Licensing &amp; Compliance Analyst. <strong>Reviewed by <a href="/about/authors#margaret-finch">Margaret Finch</a>, Finance &amp; Taxation Analyst.</strong></p>
      <p class="last-updated">Published July 21, 2026</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="answer-capsule"><strong>Yes. Recreational cannabis is legal in Maine</strong> for adults 21 and older. Maine voters legalized adult-use cannabis in 2016, and licensed retail sales began in October 2020. You can legally buy, possess, and consume cannabis within the limits set by <a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/28-b/title28-Bsec1501.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Title 28-B of the Maine Revised Statutes</a>, regulated by the Maine Office of Cannabis Policy (OCP). The main exceptions: no public consumption, no federal land, and no driving impaired.</p>

  <section>
    <h2>The Short Answer: Yes, Since 2020</h2>
    <p>Maine became one of the first states in the Northeast to legalize recreational cannabis. The 2016 ballot measure (Question 1) legalized adult possession and use, and the first licensed adult-use dispensaries opened in October 2020. Today the state has roughly 180 active adult-use retail stores.</p>
    <p>Legal cannabis in Maine covers three activities for adults 21+:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Buying</strong> from a licensed adult-use dispensary with a valid photo ID.</li>
      <li><strong>Possessing</strong> up to the legal limits (below).</li>
      <li><strong>Growing</strong> a limited number of plants at home for personal use.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Maine also has a long-running medical cannabis program (since 1999) governed by <a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/22/title22ch558-C.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Title 22 ch. 558-C</a>, which operates under separate rules and higher possession and grow limits for registered patients and caregivers.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="stat-block">
    <h3>Maine Cannabis Legality — 2026 Snapshot</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Adult-use legal since:</strong> 2016 ballot; retail sales from October 2020</li>
      <li><strong>Legal age:</strong> 21+</li>
      <li><strong>Regulator:</strong> Maine Office of Cannabis Policy (OCP)</li>
      <li><strong>Governing law:</strong> Title 28-B, Maine Revised Statutes</li>
      <li><strong>Active adult-use retail stores:</strong> ~180 (OCP 2025 annual report)</li>
      <li><strong>Retail-authorized municipalities:</strong> 78 of 485 (OCP opt-in data, refreshed 2026-07-17)</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Possession Limits in Maine</h2>
    <p>Maine sets clear possession limits for adults 21+. Staying within them keeps your possession fully legal.</p>
    <table aria-label="Maine cannabis possession limits 2026">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Product</th><th>Limit (21+)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Cannabis (flower or combination)</td><td>2.5 ounces (~70g)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cannabis concentrate (within the 2.5 oz total)</td><td>10 grams max</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Edibles (retail package)</td><td>10mg/serving, 200mg/package</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Home-produced cannabis on private property</td><td>All cannabis produced by your own plants</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>These limits come from <a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/28-b/title28-Bsec1501.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">28-B M.R.S. §1501</a>. The 10-gram concentrate cap was raised from 5 grams in 2023 under P.L. 2023, ch. 396 — many out-of-date sources still cite the old 5-gram figure. Keep cannabis in sealed, child-resistant packaging and away from children and pets. In a vehicle, store it in a closed container, ideally in the trunk.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Can You Grow Cannabis at Home in Maine?</h2>
    <p>Yes. Adults 21+ may grow cannabis at home for personal use within these limits:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Up to 6 mature flowering plants</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Up to 12 immature plants</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Unlimited seedlings</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>The mature-plant limit was raised from 3 to 6 in 2023 under P.L. 2023, ch. 220 (LD 555). The limits are per person, so a household of two adults can grow 12 mature and 24 immature plants. Plants must be tagged with your name, ID number, and the notation "Personal Adult Use – Title 28-B, Section 1502," and grown in a secure space not visible from a public way. Medical patients and registered caregivers operate under separate, higher plant limits — see our <a href="/blog/maine-home-grow-cannabis-guide-2026">Maine home grow guide</a> for the full rules.</p>
  </section>

  <Callout title="Where Cannabis Is Still Illegal in Maine" type="warning">
    <strong>Public consumption</strong> — prohibited statewide.<br/>
    <strong>Federal land</strong> — illegal in Acadia National Park, national forests, wildlife refuges, and federal buildings (cannabis is still Schedule I federally).<br/>
    <strong>Driving impaired</strong> — a cannabis OUI carries the same class of penalties as alcohol.<br/>
    <strong>Crossing state lines</strong> — transporting cannabis out of Maine is a federal offense, even to another legal state.
  </Callout>

  <section>
    <h2>Where to Buy Cannabis Legally in Maine</h2>
    <p>Maine has roughly 180 active adult-use retail stores across 78 retail-authorized municipalities. Not every town allows retail cannabis — about 407 municipalities have no recorded retail authorization — so your nearest dispensary may be in a neighboring town. Use our <a href="/find-a-dispensary">dispensary finder</a> to locate a licensed store, or browse our city-by-city dispensary guides for details on each location.</p>
    <p>For first-time buyers, our <a href="/blog/best-maine-edibles-2026">edibles buyer's guide</a> and <a href="/blog/best-maine-dispensaries-2026">best Maine dispensaries</a> roundup cover what to expect and which operators stand out.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Medical vs. Recreational in Maine</h2>
    <p>You do not need a medical card to buy cannabis in Maine, but having one changes what you can access:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Adult-use (21+, ID only):</strong> buy at licensed adult-use dispensaries; possession up to 2.5 oz flower; subject to the 14% retail cannabis sales tax effective January 1, 2026 (up from 10% under P.L. 2025, ch. 388).</li>
      <li><strong>Medical (MMMP card or reciprocity-state card):</strong> buy at registered medical dispensaries; higher possession and grow limits; the lower 5.5% state sales tax.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For the medical patient route, see our <a href="/blog/maine-medical-marijuana-patient-guide">Maine medical marijuana patient guide</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <Faq faqs={faqItems} />
  </section>

  <section class="disclaimer">
    <p><em>This article is for informational purposes only and does not constitute legal advice. Cannabis regulations change frequently. Verify current requirements with the <a href="https://www.maine.gov/dafs/ocp/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Maine Office of Cannabis Policy</a> before making any purchase or cultivation decision.</em></p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-verification">
    <div class="verification-badge">
      <span class="verification-icon">◆</span>
      <span class="verification-text"> Last reviewed <strong>2026-07-21</strong> by <strong>Calvin Waters + Margaret Finch</strong> against the OCP, the Maine Legislature, and the primary sources cited in this page. Editor-pair review on file; methodology and reviewer bios at <a href="/about/corrections">/about/corrections</a> and <a href="/about/authors">/about/authors</a>. </span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <p class="editorial-note" style="font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-text-light); margin-top: 2.5rem; padding-top: 1.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);"><strong>Editorial note.</strong> This guide is part of the Maine Dispensary Guide editorial corpus. Every material correction to this page is documented in our public <a href="/about/corrections" style="color: var(--color-primary); text-decoration: underline;">Editorial Corrections Log</a> with the primary source that confirms the fix.</p>

  <AutoRelated currentPath="/blog/is-weed-legal-in-maine" currentTopics={["policy", "legislation", "consumer-guide"]} section="Policy & Legislation" limit={6} />
</article>
</Layout>
